; coverage test for arith.kurt's factorial recursion, added alongside "factorial-base"
; (previously the only factorial fact, `0! = 1`, with no recursive step at all -- `n!` for any
; n != 0 was simply not derivable from anything). Proves `1! = 1` by chaining
; "factorial-step" + "factorial-base" + "mul-identity".
;
; note on a real limitation found while writing this: chaining a *second* application of
; "factorial-step" (e.g. to go on and prove `2! = 2`) runs into a genuine equal-elim/flat-
; operator interaction gap -- substituting a fact whose RHS is itself a `*`-expression (like
; `1! = 1 * 1`) into another `*`-context (like `2 * 1!`) fails to re-flatten and match a
; naturally-written target (`2 * (1 * 1)` flattens to `2 * 1 * 1` when typed, but the
; substituted derivation result apparently doesn't get re-flattened the same way before
; comparison). This is a real, separate, currently-undiagnosed gap in the matching engine, not
; a soundness bug (it only makes some true things harder to reach, not false things reachable)
; -- see `todo-claude.md`, flagged there for dedicated investigation rather than being
; papered over here with a proof that happens to dodge it.
load arith
use 1 - 1 = 0   "lit-1-1"
use 1 > 0

show 1! = 1
proof
    1! = 1 * (1-1)!
       = 1 * 0!
       = 1 * 1
       = 1
qed
;;; Proof checked.
